Title: "Natural Numbers"
Id: "acc9e650-ac98-4a0b-821a-5bc440a9388e"


Text: "The natural numbers $\mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}$ are the
       foundational numbers of discrete counting and order. Arising intuitively
       from the rhythm of succession, they form the archetype of inductive
       systems. Rather than treating counting numbers as unexamined primitives,
       modern foundational mathematics axiomatizes their essential dynamics: a
       single starting point and a repeatable forward progression that never
       cycles and never merges."
Id: "3079172c-5778-44bd-9391-60ce0f0b12e7"


SectionTitle: "The Peano Axioms"
Id: "1bf121c7-8539-4a95-8870-6ef558c2a240"


[\naturals]
Defines: Nb ::= (N, 0, S(n_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. N is \set
. 0 "in" N
. S is \function:on{N}:to{N}
expresses:
. forAll: n "in" N
  then: S(n) != 0
. forAll: m, n "in" N
  then: (.S(m) = S(n).) \.implies./ (.m = n.)
. forAll: A \:subset:/ N
  then:
  . if:
    . 0 "in" A
    . forAll: n "in" A
      then: S(n) "in" A
    then: A = N
Enables:
. capability: n_ "in" Nb :-> n_ is \natural
. view:
  as: X := N is \set
Documented:
. called: "the naturals"
. written: "\mathbb{N}"
. description: "The natural numbers $(\mathbb{N}, 0, S)$ axiomatized via the
                Peano postulates: $0$ is not a successor, the successor function
                $S$ is injective, and induction holds."
Id: "502e2a50-cced-461a-bdcf-03e2221a598f"


[\natural]
Declares: n "in" \naturals
Enables:
. capability: n_ + m_ :=> n_ \.natural.+./ m_
Documented:
. called: "natural"
. written: "n? \in \mathbb{N}"
. description: "A natural number is a member of the underlying set $\mathbb{N}$."
Id: "6c240df6-c06d-4fc4-ab16-31fa5aebb4d7"


SectionTitle: "Constants and Arithmetic"
Id: "d11de551-8fdb-461b-bb19-787caa038d69"


Text: "The initial natural number is zero ($0$), representing the base of the
       inductive hierarchy. The number one ($1$) is defined as its immediate
       successor, $1 := S(0)$. The successor function $S$ acts as the atomic
       step of discrete arithmetic, generating each numeral from its
       predecessor."
Id: "428700ea-91a3-4f0a-9418-1e0bd3a8c0d7"


[\natural.zero]
Defines: z := \naturals..0 is \natural
Documented:
. called: "natural zero"
. written: "0"
. description: "The distinguished element $0 \in \mathbb{N}$."
Id: "3c4f37ab-1bff-4a4a-90ea-8f808863dbfc"


[\natural.one]
Defines: o := \naturals..S(\natural.zero) is \natural
Documented:
. called: "natural one"
. written: "1"
. description: "The natural number $1$, defined as the successor $S(0)$."
Id: "8ced2d1f-741d-4996-84f8-b2c2afd293e8"


[\natural.successor:of{n}]
Defines: m := \naturals..S(n) is \natural
when: n is \natural
Documented:
. called: "successor of $n?$"
. written: "S(n?)"
. description: "The successor of the natural number $n$."
Id: "9e6169e7-03a2-4b46-8e49-9d7da97d0092"


Text: "Addition $+ : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is defined
       recursively on the first argument:
       $$\begin{aligned}
       0 + m &:= m \\
       S(k) + m &:= S(k + m)
       \end{aligned}$$
       The base clause establishes $0$ as the left identity; the recursive
       clause formalizes addition as iterated succession. Thus, computing
       $n + m$ amounts to stepping the successor function $n$ times starting
       from $m$."
Id: "f4dd2fa3-9c90-4e21-ac5e-ef4f4052e599"


[n_ \.natural.+./ m_]
Defines: n_ + m_ is \binary.operation:on{\naturals}
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: n_ = 0
  then: n_ + m_ := m_
  else:
  . let: k is \natural
    where: n_ = \naturals..S(k)
    then: n_ + m_ := \naturals..S(k \.natural.+./ m_)
Documented:
. called: "$n_?$ plus $m_?$"
. written: "n_? + m_?"
. description: "Addition of natural numbers defined by recursion on the first
                argument: $0 + m = m$ and $S(k) + m = S(k + m)$."
Id: "a8dda59b-29db-4ac2-8d70-e9010ccae3e7"


Text: "Multiplication $\cdot : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is
       defined by primitive recursion as repeated addition:
       $$\begin{aligned}
       0 \cdot m &:= 0 \\
       S(k) \cdot m &:= (k \cdot m) + m
       \end{aligned}$$
       Multiplying $n$ by $m$ computes the sum of $n$ copies of $m$, with $0$
       acting as the annihilating zero element."
Id: "2334b955-ab67-4bb6-baf5-b7f8e72cae55"


[n_ \.natural.*./ m_]
Defines: n_ * m_ is \binary.operation:on{\naturals}
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: n_ = 0
  then: n_ * m_ := 0
  else:
  . let: k is \natural
    where: n_ = \naturals..S(k)
    then: n_ * m_ := (k \.natural.*./ m_) \.natural.+./ m_
Documented:
. called: "$n_?$ times $m_?$"
. written: "n_? \cdot m_?"
. description: "Multiplication of natural numbers defined by recursion:
                $0 \cdot m = 0$ and $S(k) \cdot m = (k \cdot m) + m$."
Id: "46c21a80-12cd-45f6-8e74-07731d18d220"


SectionTitle: "Order on the Natural Numbers"
Id: "c2be9441-a886-483d-ac05-cbf63c49eb49"


Text: "The algebraic operation of addition induces the canonical linear order on
       $\mathbb{N}$. We define $n \le m$ if and only if $m$ can be reached from
       $n$ by adding some non-negative increment:
       $$n \le m \iff \exists k \in \mathbb{N}, \, n + k = m$$ This relation
       $\le$ is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive, endowing $\mathbb{N}$
       with the structure of a totally ordered cancellative commutative
       semiring."
Id: "95c0a40d-4f64-4ab4-a7c4-fbc86a103719"


[n \:natural.leq:/ m]
Declares: n is \natural
when: m is \natural
satisfies:
. exists: k is \natural
  suchThat: n \.natural.+./ k = m
Documented:
. called: "$n?$ is less than or equal to $m?$"
. written: "n? \leq m?"
. description: "The canonical order on natural numbers: $n \leq m$ if and only
                if there exists $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $n + k = m$."
Id: "8c997826-bcef-4032-816b-c6dd2aa635e7"


[\natural.initial.segment:before{n}]
Defines: I := \set@{k_ : k_ "in" \naturals | (.k_ \:natural.leq?:/ n.) \.and./ (.k_ != n.)}
when: n is \natural
Documented:
. called: "natural initial segment before $n?$"
. written: "\{0,\ldots,n?-1\}"
. description: "The set of natural numbers strictly smaller than $n$."
Id: "898f2850-dddb-43b0-809a-53b449cb04a3"
